{:nav-title "Function clauses"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Function Clauses"]
  [:p "A predicate like " [:code "[(> ?speed 100)]"] " only filters. "
   "A " [:strong "function clause"] " goes one step further: it calls a function and binds the "
   "result to a new variable, " [:code "[(function arg ...) ?result]"] ". "
   "The new variable can be used by later clauses, returned in " [:code ":find"]
   ", or grouped and counted, like any other."]

  [:h2 "Arithmetic"]
  [:p "A Pokemon's base stat total is the sum of its six base stats. "
   [:code "+"] " accepts as many arguments as we give it. "
   "Here is every Pokemon with a total of 600 or more. "
   "The first function clause binds " [:code "?total"] ", and the predicate after it filters on it:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?total
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/hp ?hp]
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]
    [?e :stat/defense ?defense]
    [?e :stat/sp-attack ?sp-attack]
    [?e :stat/sp-defense ?sp-defense]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(+ ?hp ?attack ?defense ?sp-attack ?sp-defense ?speed) ?total]
    [(>= ?total 600)]]]

  [:h2 "Watch out for integer division"]
  [:p "Dividing one integer by another drops the fractional part rather than rounding down: "
   "Datomic's " [:code "/"] " truncates toward zero, like Clojure's " [:code "quot"] ". "
   "For negative numbers that differs from a floor: " [:code "(/ -7 2)"] " is " [:code "-3"]
   ", not " [:code "-4"] ". It also isn't a ratio like Clojure's own " [:code "/"]
   " would give: dividing 11 by 9 here gives the integer " [:code "1"] ", not " [:code "11/9"] ". "
   "Multiply one side by " [:code "1.0"] " first to get a decimal. "
   "Mewtwo's attack is 110 and its defense is 90:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?whole ?ratio
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Mewtwo"]
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]
    [?e :stat/defense ?defense]
    [(/ ?attack ?defense) ?whole]
    [(* 1.0 ?attack) ?decimal]
    [(/ ?decimal ?defense) ?ratio]]]

  [:h2 "Strings"]
  [:p [:code "str"] " joins values into a string. Here it builds a label from a Pokemon's number and name:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?label
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/type "Ghost"]
    [?e :pokemon/number ?number]
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(str "#" ?number " " ?name) ?label]]]
  [:p [:code "subs"] " takes part of a string, from a start index up to (but not including) an end index. "
   "Bound values can be grouped like any variable, so we can use " [:code "count"]
   ", as in " [:a {:href "/cardinality-many"} "Cardinality many"] ", to see how many Pokemon start with each letter. "
   "Datalog groups by everything in " [:code ":find"] " that isn't an aggregate:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?initial (count ?e)
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(subs ?name 0 1) ?initial]]]
  [:p [:code "clojure.string/upper-case"] " and " [:code "clojure.string/lower-case"] " are available too."]

  [:h2 "Defaults with get-else"]
  [:p "Only a few Pokemon have a " [:code ":pokemon/category"] ". "
   "A clause that uses the attribute leaves out every Pokemon without it. "
   [:code "get-else"] " keeps them, and supplies a default value instead. "
   "It takes the database, an entity, an attribute and the default:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?category (count ?e)
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name _]
    [(get-else $ ?e :pokemon/category :regular) ?category]]]
  [:p "get-else has two limits worth knowing. The attribute must be cardinality one: asking it for "
   [:code ":pokemon/type"] " (cardinality many) refuses with " [:code "cardinality-many attrs not supported: :pokemon/type"]
   ". And the default can't be " [:code "nil"] ": that refuses too, with " [:code "nil default value not supported"]
   ". Use a sentinel value like " [:code ":none"] " instead."]
  [:p "The next chapter, " [:a {:href "/negation"} "Negation"] ", shows the opposite: "
   [:code "missing?"] " keeps only the Pokemon that lack the attribute."]

  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Lower the total in the first query to " [:code "580"] " to see the legendary birds join the list. "
    "Or wrap the label in " [:code "clojure.string/upper-case"] " with another function clause. "
    "Or change " [:code ":pokemon/category"] " to " [:code ":pokemon/type"] " in the get-else query above "
    "to see the cardinality-many error for yourself."]]]}
